# Billing and entitlements

Permanent rule for all pricing, checkout, subscription, usage, credit, and plan-gating work. Uses Stripe as the payment authority. Complements `security-and-privacy.mdc` (server-side enforcement), `trust-and-claims.mdc` (honest pricing), and `lifecycle-communications.mdc` (billing emails).

> Billing determines what the customer pays. Entitlements determine what the product allows. They must be connected, but they must not be the same implementation concern.

> A premium SaaS experience is defined as much by what happens when something goes wrong as by what happens when everything works.

## Distinct concepts

Keep these separate in code and in memory: product catalog, prices, plans, subscriptions, billing status, product entitlements, usage, credits, limits, permissions, promotions, and trials. A plan is not a price. A subscription is not an entitlement. Usage is not a permission.

## Access derives from authoritative state

Application access must be derived from authoritative server-side state (Stripe as source of truth, reconciled into the app). Do not grant paid access solely because the browser visited a Checkout success URL. The success URL is a hint, not proof. Confirm entitlement server-side before unlocking paid capability.

## Billing states to consider

New subscription, trialing, active, past due, payment failed, incomplete, paused, canceled, scheduled cancellation, grace period, expired, refunded, disputed, upgraded, downgraded, reactivated, one-time purchase, promotional access, complimentary access, usage exhaustion, credit exhaustion.

Not every product needs every state. Design the smallest set that matches the real plan structure, and mark the rest not applicable in `billing-state-model.md`.

## Required behavior

- Verified billing webhooks (signature checked before processing).
- Idempotent event processing with a stored event ID or equivalent guard.
- Duplicate-event protection.
- Safe out-of-order event handling (use event timestamps / subscription state, not arrival order).
- Server-side entitlement checks on every plan-gated action.
- Clearly defined grace-period behavior.
- Clearly defined downgrade behavior (including what happens to data over the new limit).
- Clearly defined cancellation behavior.
- Clearly defined data retention after cancellation.
- Clearly defined failed-payment recovery.
- Accurate invoice and receipt behavior.
- Clear renewal language.
- Clear upgrade and downgrade effects (timing, proration, access changes).
- No hidden billing terms, surprise charges, or misleading "free" claims.
- No permanently locked user data without explanation.
- No destructive downgrade without warning.
- No accidental entitlement leakage across workspaces or accounts.

## Every restricted interface must explain

- What is unavailable.
- Why it is unavailable.
- What plan or action unlocks it.
- What happens to existing data.
- Whether the restriction is temporary.
- Whether the attempted work was preserved.

An upgrade prompt must never hide what the user was trying to do.

## Repository-specific baseline (known facts)

- Provider: Stripe (`stripe@22`).
- Plans defined in `src/lib/stripe/plans.ts`: `starter` (10 monitors), `pro` (50 monitors), `business` (unlimited). Prices resolved by `lookup_key` (`fajita_<plan>_<interval>`). Pricing amounts are `[UNRESOLVED]` (set in Stripe Dashboard).
- Entitlements resolved in `src/lib/stripe/entitlements.ts` by reading Stripe as the source of truth (`customers.search` on `metadata.user_id`, then subscription status).
- Webhook route `src/app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts` verifies signatures and delegates to `src/lib/billing/webhook-processor.ts`, which persists org-scoped `billing_subscriptions`, writes entitlement snapshots, and idempotizes via `billing_webhook_events` (unique `stripe_event_id`). Pure inbox classification is unit-tested in `webhook-inbox.test.ts`.
- **Known launch gaps (Phase 18):** `BILLING_ENFORCEMENT_ENABLED` is still `false`; live Stripe price amounts and controlled live payment test are open blockers; route-level signature e2e remains thin (LB-009).
- Org-keyed billing tables have RLS (customer SELECT where appropriate; webhook inbox / admin tables deny-by-default for authenticated).
- Identity keys are Clerk user IDs (`user_id text`), linked to Stripe via customer metadata.

Reconcile payment-provider state and application state deliberately. Do not let the cache and Stripe drift silently; define which is authoritative for each check (Stripe authoritative; cache is a performance mirror).

## Required memory updates

- `.cursor/maturity-memory/entitlement-matrix.md`
- `.cursor/maturity-memory/billing-state-model.md`

Before implementing pricing, checkout, trials, subscriptions, credits, usage limits, one-time purchases, upgrades, downgrades, cancellation, failed-payment recovery, the billing portal, or plan-gated features, invoke `billing-and-entitlement-architect`.

## Avoid overengineering

Use Stripe Checkout, Customer Portal, and webhooks instead of custom billing UI or a custom payment ledger. Do not build a usage-metering pipeline before a metered plan exists. Prefer a small, well-defined set of states with straightforward recovery paths.

